  "worked_solution": "$$\n\nFirst, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity and rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{cosh}^{3}⁡x}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
